{"citation": "FAA, \"Thermal Exhaust Plume Impact on Airport Operations: Technical Guidance and Assessment Tool\" (based on MITRE Exhaust-Plume-Analyzer), which finds overall flight-disruption risk is low but notes plumes may pose a unique hazard to aircraft in critical phases of flight — particularly takeoff, landing, and within the pattern.", "claim": "The FAA rates the overall flight-disruption risk from thermal plumes as low, though the agency notes plumes may pose a unique hazard to aircraft in critical phases of flight — takeoff, landing, and within the pattern.", "claim_id": "C-01", "draft_sha256": "2e96b9545a4c131c5a6c8d406ebf0fe2e0fc1df4a2a85460464a52d2e20d7542", "evidence_ids": ["regulatory-political-analyst::E-0095", "operations-analyst::E-0033"], "footnote_id": "1", "primary_source_checked": true, "retained": true, "verification_note": "Draft originally said 'meaningful mainly to light aircraft in calm, cold, unstable air, not to transport-category jets.' FAA document says 'aircraft in critical phases of flight' — no aircraft-category distinction. Narrowed to what the primary source supports. E-0095 is status 'disputed'; PDF returned HTTP 403; confirmed via search summary and law-blog corroboration.", "verification_status": "corrected"}
